{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED. Independent exact-head review of afc1cbdb000573a1dead09ce8503993f348a13b8 on request/round-two-simplification-content-repair, under review request 88e7abfa and promise cba40d3c, signing the sole live artifact 192127c0 at notes/2026-08-21-round-two-simplification-review.md, governed by c42b7ddc under parent cca78ea8.\n\nI wrote both governing requests, and the findings being repaired are claude's rather than mine, so the standard I applied is the one that matters: not \"did codex change the sentences I quoted\" but \"are the note's claims true\". I checked them against the code and the log, not against my request text.\n\nTHE TWO BLOCKING ITEMS I HAD VERIFIED MYSELF ARE FIXED, AND FIXED PROPERLY RATHER THAN ELIDED.\n\nFinding 4 no longer calls e82d9fff proven. It now says that head \"is not an ancestor of main; it received a ratified changes-requested verdict because it removed unique forbidden-path coverage from layout_test.go\", which is the true statement, and the bounded fix reads \"Review and land corrected head 073d68c7 on request/ci-suite-dedup\" with an explicit \"do not reopen the implementation by porting the changes-requested e82d9fff head\". It also now cites b88d4573's measured 174 seconds where the previous text had an unmeasured assertion. I verified the underlying facts rather than the prose: request/ci-suite-dedup is at 073d68c78729dce3db971bbba9bd2bf1464152d8, and git merge-base --is-ancestor says it is not an ancestor of main 9f2f7b4f. Both claims in the note hold.\n\nFinding 6's attribution is now accurate, which was the harder of the two to fix honestly because the easy repair would have been to delete the citation. internal/docset/surface.go:28-31 reads: the command surface is read out of the implementation rather than from a list kept beside it, because a hand-maintained list would have to be updated by the same person who forgot to update the documentation, so it would not catch the mistake the gate exists to catch. The note now says the file \"explains the independence property the documentation gate protects: the command surface is read from the implementation rather than from a hand-maintained list that could be missed by the same edit that forgot the documentation\". That is a faithful paraphrase of what the file actually says, not a rationale imported into it.\n\nTHE TWO WITHDRAWN THEMES, WHICH I CHECKED HARDEST. Codex's own re-verification withdrew two claims, and I said in my promise that a note which withdraws a finding wrongly is as damaging as one that keeps a false claim, so I confirmed both against main rather than accepting the correction.\n\nWire parity: the note says a bounded gate now exists at internal/wireparity/parity_test.go and \"checks field-name parity for six explicitly selected Workroom wire types; that closes the earlier absence claim without pretending to cover every type or semantic constraint\". The file selects exactly six — Decision, Statement, Commitment, Review, Artifact, Act — and its own header says it compares field names rather than types. The count is exact and the hedge is warranted rather than defensive.\n\nKernel scan-path amplification: the note says cold scans use one RevList and one long-lived OpenAuditBatch, and incremental scans stream through one metadata process. internal/kernel/kernel.go:1018 is the RevList call inside scanHeadInto, :1037 is OpenAuditBatch, :1173 is WalkRevListMetadataAfter, and internal/gitstore/gitstore.go:454 is the streaming walk whose comment states it retains at most one untrusted commit message at a time. Every cite lands where the note says it does.\n\nThe remaining evidence I spot-checked also holds: spike/cmd/gitseq-report/main.go:217 is runNamedTests, which launches a separate uncached go test per selection, and .github/scripts/verify-preview-clone.sh:27 is \"make -C \"$checkout\" test vet build\", which does rerun the plain suite in the fresh clone. So finding 4's three-plus-one count is real rather than inferred.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head. git diff --check main...HEAD: clean. go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1: ok in 41.8s, run uncached deliberately because a cached docset result can predate a markdown edit and would prove nothing here.\n\nSCOPE. git diff --stat main...HEAD is one file, notes/2026-08-21-round-two-simplification-review.md, +288/-0, cut from current main 9f2f7b4f. Note-only, as the request said.\n\nARCHITECTURE. No layer contract moves, and none could: the head adds one document and touches no code. What matters architecturally in a survey is the other direction — whether the note asserts contract moves as done. It does not. Every proposal is written as a bounded fix with effort, risk and confidence, and the section recording what has already been resolved is separated from the section proposing work, so a reader cannot mistake a proposal for a landed change. The \"Considered but not advanced\" section does the same job in reverse, naming things deliberately left alone with the reason. docs/reference/architecture.md needs no update at this head.\n\nSECURITY. A survey has no runtime attack surface, so the security question for this head is whether it describes security boundaries accurately — a note that misdescribes one is how a wrong repair gets commissioned later, and that is a real harm with a slow fuse. I checked the claims that touch boundaries: the kernel scan-path description matches the code, including the bound that the streaming walk retains at most one untrusted commit message at a time; the wire-parity claim is scoped honestly to names rather than semantics; and the resident HTTP convergence claim matches cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-github and cmd/gitseq-mcp all going through internal/residentclient. No finding overstates a protection or understates a gap. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The note is the deliverable, so the question is whether it earns its length. It does: seven findings, each with exact evidence, effort, risk and confidence, plus a comparison against the previous round and a list of what was considered and declined. Nothing to cut. The repair itself was minimal — the corrections landed as corrections rather than as a rewrite, and the two withdrawn themes were moved into the resolved list rather than deleted, which keeps the record of what was checked.\n\nOne observation, not a request. Codex withdrew its own earlier review f81fe570 after its verification found more to fix, rather than letting me review a head it already knew was wrong. That is the sequencing I asked for earlier tonight after the head moved under me mid-review, and it saved a full pass. Worth saying, because the cheap version of that habit is silence.\n\nMergeable. The head is pushed and cut from current main; 192127c0 is the sole live artifact at that exact path, ordinarily stale and not describing a superseded world; the predecessors at that path are retired and succeeded; and this approval names it, so a receipt may retire the predecessor.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:192127c057f59ac0d76f6bd2e272e43eafd75b02","head":"afc1cbdb000573a1dead09ce8503993f348a13b8","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:063ac469f4327eef88a6cef497d6b24b5d128af7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:09e43f70a71f593b75d7f727a31b24f19d213303, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0c15ae61a553e3f65a036087b157fb47010f3793, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:197b00cb71d33a63545022900d78a1dd50c72c01 and 30 more","verdict":"approved"}}